==English==
{{wp|Wife (disambiguation)}}

===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|id=woman|inh|enm>wyf>woman|tree=1}}
{{inh+|en|enm|wyf}}, {{m|enm|wif}}, from {{inh|en|ang|wīf|t=woman, wife}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*wīb}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wībą|t=woman, wife}}.

Germanic cognates include {{cog|sco|wife||wife}}, {{cog|fy|wiif||wife, woman}}, {{cog|stq|Wieuw||woman, lady, female}}, {{cog|frr|wüf||wife, woman}}, {{cog|nl|wijf||woman, female}}, {{cog|nds|Wief||woman, female}}, {{cog|de|Weib||woman, wife, female}}, {{cog|da|viv||wife, woman}}, {{cog|no|viv||wife, woman, girl}}, {{cog|sv|viv||woman}}, {{cog|fo|vív||wife, woman}}, {{cog|is|víf||woman}}.

The further etymology is unknown, with a number of disputed suggestions. One suggestion connects Tocharian A/B {{m|xto|kip}}/{{m|txb|kwīpe||genitals, female pudenda}}, for a hypothetical Indo-European {{m|ine-pro|*gʰwíbʰ-|t=pudenda}}.<ref>{{R:txb:Adams 1999|page=238}}</ref><ref>Klaus Totila Schmit and Klaus Strunk, “Toch. B ''kwī̆pe'' ‘Schaum, Schande’, A ''kip'' ‘Schaum’ und germ. *''wīƀa'' ‘Weib’”, ''Indogermanica Europaea: Festschrift für Wolfgang Meid'' (Graz: Institut für Sprachwissenschaft der Universität Graz, 1989), pages 251-284</ref>
Another suggestion connects Old English {{m|ang|wǣfan||wrap, clothe}}, Old Norse {{m|non|vífa||wrap, veil}} for a suggested original motive of "married woman wearing a scarf".

Yet another suggestion  connects Old High German {{m|goh|weibōn||move to and fro}}, Old Norse {{m|non|veifa||swing, throw}}, for a motive of "one who is moving busily; housekeeper, maidservant" (cf. German {{m|de|Weibel||manservant, usher}}).

